<h1>1. ABSTRACT &amp; EXECUTIVE SUMMARY</h1>
<p>
Modern multi-terminal retail setups require sub-second inventory synchronization and absolute transactional integrity. Conventional Point-of-Sale (POS) software architectures exhibit significant design vulnerabilities: cashiers operate on decoupled terminals while administrative screens rely on polling mechanisms to detect inventory changes. Under flash-sale scenarios or rapid barcode checkout queues, naive read-then-write logic permits multiple cashiers to sell the last remaining unit of an item concurrently, resulting in negative inventory states (overselling).
</p>
<p>
This capstone project presents the design, implementation, and empirical verification of the <strong>Real-Time Inventory &amp; Billing System</strong>, an enterprise-grade retail platform uniting three distinct client-server computing tiers:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>WPF Cashier Terminal (MVVM):</strong> A hardware-accelerated retail point-of-sale desktop interface built with pure Model-View-ViewModel architecture, reactive cart calculations, barcode scanning, and instant receipt rendering.</li>
  <li><strong>Windows Forms Admin Console:</strong> A high-density administrative operations center featuring real-time financial KPI cards, a Live Sales Ticker updating in under 15ms via WebSockets, product catalog CRUD, and inventory replenishment controls.</li>
  <li><strong>ASP.NET Core Web API Backend:</strong> A microservice application server incorporating JSON Web Token (JWT) role-based authorization and a persistent SignalR duplex WebSocket hub.</li>
  <li><strong>SQL Server Relational Engine:</strong> Enforces strict ACID transactions, stored procedures with pessimistic row-level locking (<code>UPDLOCK, ROWLOCK</code>), and covering B-Tree indexes.</li>
</ol>

