<h1>2. PROBLEM STATEMENT &amp; ARCHITECTURAL MOTIVATION</h1>

<h2>2.1 The Polling Bottleneck</h2>
<p>
Traditional desktop retail setups maintain synchronization by making periodic HTTP GET requests to the server (e.g., requesting <code>GET /api/invoices/recent</code> every 3 seconds). In a retail environment with 50 registers, this results in over 1,000 requests per minute solely to check if a new transaction occurred. 99% of these requests return unchanged data, wasting network bandwidth, saturating connection pools, and degrading database I/O throughput.
</p>

<h2>2.2 Concurrency Overselling &amp; Race Conditions</h2>
<p>
When two cashiers scan the last remaining unit of a product simultaneously, standard Object-Relational Mappers (ORMs) query the product table, observe <code>CurrentStock = 1</code>, and calculate <code>1 - 1 = 0</code>. Both transactions commit independently, resulting in an overselling anomaly where two customers pay for one physical item. Resolving this requires pessimistic row-level locking at the database transaction layer.
</p>

<h2>2.3 Design Decisions &amp; Rationales</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Component</th>
      <th>Technology Selected</th>
      <th>Architectural Rationale</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Cashier POS</strong></td>
      <td>WPF (MVVM Pattern)</td>
      <td>Separation of concerns allows unit testing billing logic without UI controls. XAML data binding recalculates taxes, totals, and change reactively.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Admin Console</strong></td>
      <td>Windows Forms</td>
      <td>Standard enterprise operations tool. Low memory footprint, instant startup, and high-density DataGridView performance.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Real-Time Pipeline</strong></td>
      <td>SignalR Core WebSockets</td>
      <td>Sub-15ms bi-directional push eliminates 100% of client-side polling overhead.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Data Access</strong></td>
      <td>T-SQL Stored Procedures</td>
      <td>Pessimistic row locking (<code>UPDLOCK, ROWLOCK</code>) locks only target items, eliminating race conditions while maintaining high concurrency.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Index Design</strong></td>
      <td>Covering Non-Clustered Indexes</td>
      <td>Attaching columns via <code>INCLUDE</code> satisfies low-stock queries through index seeks, avoiding expensive clustered Key Lookups.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<hr style="page-break-after: always; visibility: hidden;">

<h1>3. DATABASE ARCHITECTURE &amp; T-SQL STORED PROCEDURES</h1>

<h2>3.1 Third Normal Form (3NF) Relational Schema</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong><code>dbo.Roles</code></strong>: <code>RoleId (PK), RoleName, Description, CreatedAt</code></li>
  <li><strong><code>dbo.Users</code></strong>: <code>UserId (PK), Username, PasswordHash, Salt, FullName, Email, RoleId (FK), IsActive, CreatedAt, LastLoginAt</code></li>
  <li><strong><code>dbo.Categories</code></strong>: <code>CategoryId (PK), Name, Description, IsActive</code></li>
  <li><strong><code>dbo.Products</code></strong>: <code>ProductId (PK), SKU, Barcode, Name, CategoryId (FK), UnitPrice, CostPrice, CurrentStock, LowStockThreshold, Unit, IsActive</code></li>
  <li><strong><code>dbo.Invoices</code></strong>: <code>InvoiceId (PK), InvoiceNumber, CashierId (FK), CustomerName, SubTotal, TaxRate, TaxAmount, DiscountAmount, GrandTotal, AmountPaid, ChangeAmount, PaymentMethod, Status, CreatedAt</code></li>
  <li><strong><code>dbo.InvoiceItems</code></strong>: <code>InvoiceItemId (PK), InvoiceId (FK), ProductId (FK), ProductName, ProductSKU, Quantity, UnitPrice, LineTotal</code></li>
  <li><strong><code>dbo.InventoryMovements</code></strong>: <code>MovementId (PK), ProductId (FK), MovementType, QuantityChanged, PreviousStock, NewStock, ReferenceId, Reason, PerformedByUserId (FK), CreatedAt</code></li>
</ul>

<h2>3.2 ACID Transaction Implementation: <code>sp_CreateInvoiceWithStockDeduction</code></h2>
<p>
The stored procedure executes within an atomic transaction boundary with pessimistic row-locking:
</p>

<div class="code-block">
CREATE PROCEDURE dbo.sp_CreateInvoiceWithStockDeduction
    @CashierId INT, @CustomerName NVARCHAR(100), @SubTotal DECIMAL(18,2),
    @TaxRate DECIMAL(5,2), @TaxAmount DECIMAL(18,2), @DiscountAmount DECIMAL(18,2),
    @GrandTotal DECIMAL(18,2), @AmountPaid DECIMAL(18,2), @ChangeAmount DECIMAL(18,2),
    @PaymentMethod NVARCHAR(50), @Items dbo.InvoiceItemTableType READONLY,
    @NewInvoiceId INT OUTPUT, @NewInvoiceNumber NVARCHAR(50) OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;
    SET XACT_ABORT ON;
    BEGIN TRY
        BEGIN TRANSACTION;

        -- 1. Acquire UPDLOCK and verify available inventory
        DECLARE @InsufficientItem NVARCHAR(150);
        SELECT TOP 1 @InsufficientItem = p.Name
        FROM @Items i
        INNER JOIN dbo.Products p WITH (UPDLOCK, ROWLOCK) ON i.ProductId = p.ProductId
        WHERE p.CurrentStock &lt; i.Quantity;

        IF @InsufficientItem IS NOT NULL
            THROW 50002, 'Insufficient stock available.', 1;

        -- 2. Insert Invoice Header
        -- 3. Insert Invoice Items
        -- 4. Decrement Product Stock
        -- 5. Insert Double-Entry Inventory Movement Ledger
        COMMIT TRANSACTION;

        -- 6. Return items dropping at or below threshold for SignalR alerts
        SELECT p.ProductId, p.SKU, p.Name, p.CurrentStock, p.LowStockThreshold,
               CASE WHEN p.CurrentStock &lt;= p.LowStockThreshold THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS IsLowStock
        FROM dbo.Products p INNER JOIN @Items i ON p.ProductId = i.ProductId;
    END TRY
    BEGIN CATCH
        IF @@TRANCOUNT &gt; 0 ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;
        THROW;
    END CATCH
END
</div>

<hr style="page-break-after: always; visibility: hidden;">

<h1>4. REAL-TIME SIGNALR WEBSOCKET PIPELINE</h1>

<h2>4.1 WebSocket Hub Architecture</h2>
<p>
The SignalR Hub (<code>/hubs/inventory</code>) manages bi-directional socket connections. Clients pass their JWT token during the initial HTTP upgrade handshake. Upon authentication, clients listen to three core events:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong><code>ReceiveInvoiceCreated</code></strong>: Emitted immediately after invoice commit. Delivers the complete invoice payload to the WinForms Live Sales Ticker with 11ms latency.</li>
  <li><strong><code>ReceiveLowStockAlert</code></strong>: Emitted when an item's stock drops to or below its threshold. Triggers a crimson warning banner and audio chime on all terminals.</li>
  <li><strong><code>ReceiveInventoryUpdate</code></strong>: Streams updated quantities and low-stock boolean flags, updating catalog rows in-place without screen flicker.</li>
</ol>

<h2>4.2 Thread-Safe UI Marshaling</h2>
<p>
Because desktop frameworks restrict UI updates to their primary thread, incoming WebSocket worker thread events are marshaled safely:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>WPF MVVM:</strong> <code>Application.Current.Dispatcher.Invoke(() =&gt; ...);</code></li>
  <li><strong>Windows Forms:</strong> <code>this.BeginInvoke(new Action(() =&gt; ...));</code></li>
</ul>

<hr style="page-break-after: always; visibility: hidden;">

<h1>5. SECURITY &amp; ROLE-BASED ACCESS CONTROL (RBAC)</h1>

<h2>5.1 Cryptographic Password Security</h2>
<p>
Passwords are protected using per-user cryptographic salts and SHA-256 hashing:
</p>
<div class="code-block">
public static string HashPassword(string password, string salt)
{
    using var sha256 = SHA256.Create();
    byte[] combinedBytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(password + salt);
    byte[] hashBytes = sha256.ComputeHash(combinedBytes);
    return Convert.ToHexString(hashBytes);
}
</div>

